er_play":0.3626,"pass_rate":0.6508,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5918,"def_epa_per_play":0.285},{"week":18,"opponent":"DET","home":true,"pf":16.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":54,"epa_per_play":-0.01936,"pass_rate":0.6481,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5,"def_epa_per_play":0.1443}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"DET","distance":0.453,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Ben Johnson"},{"season":2024,"team":"DET","distance":0.5738,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Ben Johnson"},{"season":2022,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5768,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2022,"team":"CAR","distance":0.6066,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"TEN","distance":0.6749,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6553,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2020,"team":"DET","distance":0.6723,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.6856,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2021,"team":"DAL","distance":0.7159,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dan Quinn"},{"season":2024,"team":"WAS","distance":0.7717,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears went 11-6 in 2025 and outscored opponents 441 to 415. The offense carried the team, ranking 8th in EPA per play while the defense finished 19th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd highest play-action rate and the 2nd lowest Cover 4 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd highest play-action rate and the 2nd lowest 21 personnel usage in the league. Caleb Williams ran the passing game, with Rome Odunze leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bears defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 29th. Montez Sweat led the team with 10 sacks, and Austin Booker added 4.5, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest Cover 4 rate in the league.","special":"The Bears were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 55% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 85% of field goals, which was about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 7% of the money on the books.","players":"Caleb Williams played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,942 yards. Targets were spread around, with Rome Odunze and DJ Moore leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by D'Andre Swift and Kyle Monangai. On defense, Montez Sweat led the team with 10 sacks and Kevin Byard picked off 7 passes.","coaching":"Ben Johnson called the offense as head coach and Dennis Allen called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Lions and the 2024 Lions, both of which also had Ben Johnson calling plays. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2020 Lions.","trends":"The offense went from 26th in EPA per play in 2024 to 8th in 2025, a big step forward. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 19th in 2025, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was play-action rate, which went from 18% to 32%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}